**Convention (Workload inputs)** · `conv:harness:workload-benchmark-inputs` Component curves measure a declared deterministic recipe through public constructors and validators. Correctness is checked before sampling and after the timed series; input construction is excluded, while allocations made by the operation and destruction of its returned owned output remain timed. Consuming marker operations receive a fresh input clone outside each timed iteration. Fixture proof equality measures structural evidence handling, not cryptographic verification or chain finality. A refused, unsupported or incomplete point remains an explicitly typed result and cannot be fitted as successful throughput. Source, configuration, dimensions, limits, setup, raw samples and resource scope accompany every measurement. Each campaign states its resource scopes. The earlier campaign has container cgroup readings only; the all-components campaign additionally records node-wide pressure, load and busy cores from proc, separately from its container cgroup. Waited process accounting gives own CPU. Neither scope attributes work to another login. Every float in the authored carrier uses exponent-free decimal notation, expanding exactly the digits of its shortest round-trip representation. The writer preserves the parsed value, including signed zero; the reader refuses other spellings. Every retained value parses back identically to the campaign raw result, as checked by the round-trip witness. The generated dataset carries those same values in the documents serializer's canonical form; its spelling need not match the authored input. The input rule prevents ambiguous digit runs without rounding evidence or changing the serializer. These curves declare no host workload, production API limit or capacity. Repeated correspondence checks reuse parsed and validated workload rows only when the complete input text is identical. The invocation rereads the file and returns a private projection; any changed byte is a different cache key and is validated afresh. The in-process cache is bounded. An owned target artifact may retain the validating producer's result across invocations only when the complete input text, complete validator source and Python version are identical. A miss revalidates before an atomic replacement; an absent or malformed artifact is a miss. This is direct byte equality under (`case:identity:no-identity`), not a digest or an independently authored approval. The proposal to hash this validation input stops at direct comparison on 2026-11-07 under (`req:identity:stop-record`): a hash would shorten the comparison key but add no decision here, because complete committed bytes are available. Retake the identity walk if a consumer must reuse evidence without those bytes or across an independently administered artifact boundary. The artifact is the validator's owned output, never a second authored evidence carrier. The gate reads the committed input snapshot and owns this cache output. No live shared checkout is policed. Test fixture compaction similarly reuses only identical source text and writes private files. All samples remain committed in the carrier and deterministic dataset, with no rendering arithmetic or uncommitted evidence locator. Cold validation and byte reading remain linear in evidence size. Repeated correspondence fixtures reuse the optimized command and cache lexical edition matches by complete text, while rereading each private fixture and recomputing path-sensitive refusals. Changed sample bytes must still agree with the deterministic dataset; changing the cache key never authorizes a stale projection. Neither raw evidence nor dataset samples are dropped. Runner declaration metadata projects policy and grid rows only; prior campaigns are evidence, not declarations to repeat recursively in the next campaign. This projection still validates the complete carrier before selecting its rows.

**Record (Sampling contract)** · `rec:workload:sampling` Full campaigns use {{ policy.number_forms.samples | display }} samples, {{ policy.number_forms.warmup_ms | display }} ms warm-up, {{ policy.number_forms.measurement_ms | display }} ms measurement targets and {{ policy.number_forms.resamples | display }} statistical resamples, with {{ policy.sampling }} iteration scheduling. The profile is {{ policy.profile }} and the deterministic seed is {{ policy.number_forms.seed | display }}. These are sampling targets. Each current grid also declares an invocation wall bound: expiration retains an incomplete operational point, with no fitted samples, rather than a mathematical FAIL. The bound includes setup, correctness, warm-up and sampling; it does not borrow a gate budget. A serialized pilot precedes a full series. The existing foundation smoke retains its settings; the extra smoke points are declared in the grid carrier. Full campaigns run serially at idle priority on a recorded allowed CPU with a separate build target. Timing is nonverdict under (`rule:devperf:nonverdict`). The dataset names each package-owned construction control. Result association is checked by (`claim:harness:workload-result-binding`).

